<body>
<h1>Unit 16: Refraction through Prisms</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Waves &amp; Optics</p><p class="summary">A prism is a device that separates wavelengths as it bends them. The unit connects geometry to spectroscopy: the apex angle sets the deviation, the refractive index sets the dispersion, and the spectrum is the primary chemical and astronomical fingerprint of light.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>A prism deviates light because its faces are not parallel, and it disperses light because the refractive index depends on wavelength - normally n is larger for blue than for red.</li><li>For a thin prism of small apex angle A, the deviation is approximately d = (n - 1) A.</li><li>The dispersive power w = (n_v - n_r)/(n_y - 1) is nearly constant for a given material, which is why glasses can be sorted into crown and flint families.</li><li>Minimum deviation occurs when the ray path is symmetric (i = e), giving n = sin((A + d_m)/2) / sin(A/2) - the standard laboratory method.</li><li>A hollow prism filled with a liquid measures that liquid's refractive index by the same minimum-deviation method.</li><li>The rainbow is refraction plus one internal reflection plus dispersion in water droplets, which is why the primary bow has an angle of about 42 degrees.</li><li>At minimum deviation the ray path is symmetric, so the refractive index can be measured without knowing the prism's apex angle precisely - which is why the spectrometer method is the reference technique.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The thin-prism approximation requires a small apex angle; thick prisms need the exact two-surface treatment with Snell's law applied twice.</li><li>Dispersion is small - the index changes by only about 0.01 to 0.02 across the visible for glass - so a single prism gives a spectrum whose orders overlap and may need a second prism to isolate.</li><li>Deviation depends on the refractive index of the surrounding medium: a prism in water disperses far less than the same prism in air.</li><li>Refractive index depends on temperature as well as wavelength, so precision spectroscopy requires controlling the temperature of the prism.</li><li>The small-angle formula n = 1 + A/2D is an approximation that fails beyond about 10 degrees of deviation; real work uses the exact minimum-deviation relation.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 3 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Minimum deviation condition</li><li>Relation between the angle of prism, minimum deviation and refractive index</li><li>Deviation in small-angle prism</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“A prism deviates light because it slows the light down.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Slowing alone is not a deviation: the ray would resume its original direction on leaving, and a parallel-sided slab does exactly that. What bends the ray is the change of direction at each face, because the faces are not parallel.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> At the first face the ray bends towards the normal (r &lt; i) and at the second face, where it goes from dense to rare, it bends away from the normal. Because the two faces are inclined at the apex angle A, the outgoing ray is displaced by the deviation delta, and for a thin prism delta = (n - 1) A.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The angle of deviation of a prism is fixed, whatever the angle of incidence.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The deviation varies with incidence, and it has a minimum value when the path through the prism is symmetric. Using the deviation for an arbitrary incidence is the source of most errors.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> The deviation is a function of i and e, with i + e = A + delta. It is smallest for i = e, the symmetric path, and that minimum is the one tabulated. At minimum deviation n = sin((A + delta_m)/2) / sin(A/2).</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The dispersive power of a glass is the difference between its refractive indices for violet and red light.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> That difference is a useful measure of dispersion but it is not the dimensionless dispersive power, which is normalised so that glasses can be compared fairly.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> omega = (n_v - n_r) / (n_y - 1). For a crown glass with n_v = 1.530, n_r = 1.514 and n_y = 1.517, the difference n_v - n_r = 0.016 while the dispersive power is 0.016 / 0.517 = 0.031.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>A prism of apex angle 60 degrees and refractive index 1.5 is used in the minimum-deviation position. Calculate the angle of minimum deviation, and verify the relation n = sin((A + delta_m)/2) / sin(A/2).</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> n = sin((A + delta_m)/2) / sin(A/2) gives 1.5 = sin((60 + delta_m)/2) / sin 30 degrees = 2 sin((60 + delta_m)/2), so sin((60 + delta_m)/2) = 0.75. Hence (60 + delta_m)/2 = 48.6 degrees and delta_m = 37.2 degrees (about 37 degrees). Substitution back gives sin(48.6)/sin(30) = 0.750/0.500 = 1.50, confirming the relation.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Derive the approximate deviation of a thin prism, delta = (n - 1) A.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> For a thin prism the angle of incidence i and the angle of emergence e are both small, and the refracted angles inside are r1 and r2 with r1 + r2 = A. From Snell's law at the first face, sin i = n sin r1, and since the angles are small, i = n r1. Similarly e = n r2. The total deviation is delta = (i - r1) + (e - r2) = (n - 1)(r1 + r2) = (n - 1) A. The result is independent of the angle of incidence, which is why a thin prism can be used as a spectrometer element: a thicker prism is dispersive but also strongly deviating.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>A hollow prism with two parallel faces at 60 degrees is filled with water and used in the minimum-deviation position. Explain why the same instrument can measure the refractive index of a liquid, and state what the measurement gives.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> The hollow prism is an optical cell whose interior can be filled with any liquid; the glass walls only serve to hold the liquid and to define the two refracting faces at the apex angle A. The light passes through the liquid exactly as it would through a solid prism of the same apex angle, so the measured minimum deviation satisfies n_liquid = sin((A + delta_m)/2) / sin(A/2). With A = 60 degrees and a measured delta_m of 44.8 degrees, n = sin(52.4)/sin(30) = 0.792/0.500 = 1.58... more precisely, using the standard value for water near 1.333 the expected deviation is 2 sin^-1(n sin 30) - 60 = 2 x 41.8 - 60 = 23.6 degrees, and the measured deviation gives the liquid's index directly. The method needs a narrow slit and a spectrometer, since the deviation must be measured precisely.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Typical prism apex angle</td><td>60 degrees</td></tr><tr><td>Primary rainbow angle</td><td>about 42 degrees</td></tr><tr><td>Dispersive power of crown glass</td><td>about 0.016</td></tr><tr><td>Refractive index of diamond</td><td>about 2.42</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>How do modern observatories push spectroscopy to fainter objects than the classical prism or grating allowed?</b></p><p class="status">Engineering frontier</p><p>Very large telescopes, échelle spectrographs and interferometry have extended the reach, and each gain is limited by photon collection and detector noise.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can a dispersive element be built that disperses without deviating, or deviates without dispersing?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Compound and metamaterial designs approach these limits, but causality constrains how well an achromatic, deviation-free element can behave over a finite bandwidth.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Given the deviation angles of red and violet light through a 60 degree prism, find the two refractive indices and hence the dispersive power.</li><li>Measure the angle of minimum deviation for a triangular glass prism, compute n, and repeat with a hollow prism filled with water.</li><li>Explain geometrically why the deviation of a prism does not grow in proportion to the thickness of the glass.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/refraction-through-prisms">Read the chapter notes: Refraction through Prisms</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-prism">Prism 3D lab</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-th-optics">Optics theory lab</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 3</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/science-overview/science-explainers/spectroscopy-101-types-of-spectra-and-spectroscopy/">NASA: Spectroscopy 101</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/1-5-dispersion">OpenStax: Dispersion</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/4-4-diffraction-gratings">OpenStax: Diffraction Gratings</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
